<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bruneck</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brunico</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bruneck, in Italian Brunico, is the main town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puster-valley">Puster Valley</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was founded in the 13th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>, who built its castle and held the town until 1803. It is now a market and tourist centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bruneck.jpg" alt="Bruneck" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolkenkratzer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I-Bruneck_008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bruneck, in Italian Brunico, is the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puster-valley">Puster Valley</a> in the Italian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>. It stands in a wide valley at the confluence of the Ahr and the Rienz, about 35 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> and 70 km from the regional capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>. To the south rises the Kronplatz massif, part of the Dolomites, with a popular ski area. According to the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, 78.71 percent of the population speak German as their first language, 19.31 percent Italian and 1.98 percent Ladin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puster-valley">Puster Valley</a> was the main road connecting northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> area. In 1091 the countship of Pustrissa passed from Henry IV to Bishop Altvino of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>. The town was probably named after its founder, the prince-bishop Bruno von Kirchberg, and appears as Bruneke in a deed of 23 February 1256, when it consisted of two rows of houses forming a narrow lane. In the turbulent interregnum after the death of Emperor Frederick II in 1250 the prince-bishop had a fortress built above the town, first mentioned in 1276. Prince-Bishop Albert von Enn enlarged it and completed the town walls and moat by 1336.</p><p>In the 14th and 15th centuries brisk trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> passed through the valley, and goods were often stored in Bruneck, bringing prosperity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puster-valley">Puster Valley</a> painting school was founded then, and the masters Michael and Friedrich Pacher studied there. In 1500 the valley was reunited with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> through a contract between the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Görz</a>, but the town remained an episcopal possession. On 11 April 1723 the worst fire in its history destroyed most of the town. After the First World War Bruneck became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> industrial zones and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> stores brought growth, and in the 1960s tourism led to many new hotels. The town depends on manufacturing and services, and the Kronplatz ski resort is its main attraction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bruneck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishopric retained Brixen, <b>Bruneck</b> and scattered lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-brixen">Prince-Bishopric of Brixen</a></li></ul></section>
